# Part 2 — Individual Long Turn — IELTS Speaking (Qatar)

Part 2 of the IELTS Speaking test is the **Individual Long Turn**: the candidate
receives a task card with a topic and three to four bullet prompts, has **1 minute
to prepare** (a pencil and paper are provided), and then speaks for **1 to 2
minutes** on the topic. The examiner does not interrupt; the candidate must produce
a continuous, structured monologue.

This is the **highest-weight topic** in the ielts-speaking subject (weight 5),
and the one most candidates prepare least for. The reason: many candidates think
"2 minutes" sounds short, but a fully-developed 2-minute monologue on an
unfamiliar topic — produced under exam pressure, in front of a stranger, with no
chance to re-do it — is one of the harder tasks in the entire IELTS test.

Part 2 is the part that is most exposed in the Qatari cohort, where candidates
often have strong English reading and writing skills (because of the Qatari
educational system) but limited practice in sustained, unrehearsed spoken English.
The 1-minute preparation time is therefore the most important 60 seconds of the
Speaking test, and how you use it determines whether your 2-minute monologue is
structured or scattered.

This note shows the LCA (Long-Read Cohort Appetite) version: a **Lite**
(5-minute) read, a **Standard** (15-minute) read, and the **Exam Essentials**
table a Qatari candidate can use to revise in the last hour before the test.

## Lite (4-Step Method)

1. **Use the 1-minute prep time to write a quick structure.** You are given a
   pencil and paper. Spend the first 10 seconds reading the topic and bullets,
   the next 20 seconds writing a 1-word opener for each bullet, the next 20
   seconds thinking of one concrete example or detail for each bullet, and the
   last 10 seconds writing a closing sentence. You will NOT have time to write
   full sentences — your notes will be 5–7 words total.
2. **Open with a single sentence that paraphrases the topic.** Do not start with
   *"I want to talk about…"* — the examiner has heard that a thousand times. Start
   with a rephrased topic (*"I'd like to describe a teacher who really influenced
   me when I was at school in Doha"*). This shows lexical range and saves you 5
   seconds.
3. **Address EVERY bullet, in order, with one developed point each.** A
   common trap: candidates talk for 90 seconds on the first bullet and 15
   seconds on the next two. The examiner scores your coverage of the task. If
   you skip a bullet, you lose marks even if your English is fluent.
4. **Close with a one-sentence summary or reflection.** Do not stop mid-thought.
   End with a phrase like *"…and that's why this person really mattered to me"*
   or *"…so it was a memorable event in my life"*. The closing sentence signals
   that you controlled the task and ran out of time on your own terms.

**Insight: Part 2 is a writing task delivered orally.** The structure that
gets you a high band score is the same structure you'd use in a written essay:
introduction, two to four developed points (one per bullet), conclusion. Treat
your 1-minute prep as outlining an essay.

### 6 Examiner Traps (Part 2 — Individual Long Turn)

- **The bullet trap** — the task card has three to four bullets; many
  candidates address only the first two. The examiner scores your coverage of
  all bullets. If you skip one, you lose marks.
- **The premature stop** — candidates speak for 60–90 seconds, run out of
  ideas, and stop. The examiner will NOT prompt you to keep going. You must
  cover the full 1–2 minutes on your own.
- **The repetition trap** — candidates repeat the same phrase (*"it was very
  very interesting"*) to fill time. Repetition is not what the examiner is
  scoring — range and accuracy are.
- **The memorized answer trap** — candidates who memorize a "speech" sound
  robotic and lose marks for pronunciation and lexical range. The examiner is
  trained to detect rehearsed answers.
- **The no-example trap** — abstract answers (*"teachers are very important
  because education is important"*) score low. You need a **specific
  example**: *the teacher who taught me biology at QU prep school and inspired
  me to study medicine*.
- **The structure-less trap** — candidates speak in one long run-on sentence,
  without paragraph-style breaks. Use discourse markers (*first of all, what I
  mean by that, in addition, finally*) to signal structure.

### Qatari-Context Matches (2025–2026 cohort signal)

In recent IELTS Speaking tests used in Qatar (and the broader Gulf), Part 2 cue
cards commonly anchor on topics relevant to Gulf students:

- **A teacher / mentor who influenced you** — common in Qatari cohorts; many
  candidates draw on QU or secondary-school teachers.
- **A place in your hometown you often visit** — common across Gulf cohorts.
- **A traditional event / celebration** — common; Qatari candidates draw on
  National Day, Garangao, Ramadan, Eid al-Fitr.
- **A piece of technology you use daily** — common; candidates draw on
  smartphones, laptops, smart-home devices.
- **An achievement you are proud of** — common; candidates draw on academic,
  sports, or community achievements.
- **A trip you remember well** — common; candidates draw on family trips
  to Turkey, Egypt, or Europe, or internal travel within Qatar.

The Qatari *Educational Reform* (Ministry of Education and Higher Education)
frequently uses IELTS-equivalent Speaking topics in Year 12 / Year 13 English
classes, which is why these topics appear so often in the Qatari cohort.

## Standard (7-Step Method with Long-Form Guidance)

### Step 1 — Read the task card and bullets (10 seconds)

You have 1 minute of preparation time, but the first 10 seconds should be spent
**just reading**. Do not write anything yet. Read the topic, read every bullet,
and let your brain absorb the task.

### Step 2 — Brainstorm an opener (20 seconds)

Think of one short opening sentence that paraphrases the topic. This is your
"topic sentence" — the equivalent of an essay's thesis statement. It should
be 8–15 words, in your own words, and not a memorized phrase.

### Step 3 — Write a 1-word cue for each bullet (20 seconds)

For each bullet on the task card, write ONE word that captures the example or
detail you will use. Example: if the bullet says *"what this teacher looked
like"*, your 1-word cue might be *"tall glasses"*. The cue triggers your
memory when you are speaking.

### Step 4 — Brainstorm a closing sentence (10 seconds)

Plan a single closing sentence — something that wraps up the topic and signals
to the examiner that you are in control. This is your "conclusion sentence".

### Step 5 — Speak the opener (5–10 seconds)

Open with the sentence you planned. Speak clearly, at a natural pace. Do not
rush.

### Step 6 — Address each bullet in order (90–110 seconds)

For each bullet, develop the point: 20–30 seconds per bullet, with the 1-word
cue triggering a concrete example. Use discourse markers (*first of all, what
I mean by that, in addition, finally*) to signal structure.

### Step 7 — Close with the planned sentence (5–10 seconds)

End with the closing sentence you planned. This signals that you controlled
the task and ran out of time on your own terms.

